For a chance to be one of two lucky people
who'll win this very cool The Art of Cars

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:35.410
3 hardback book featuring Pixar's fabulous
concept art, storyboards, and insights into

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:40.050
the movie, all you need to do is subscribe
and leave a comment about the film, and also

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.920
answer the question I'll be asking later in
the video.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.940
Many years of work go into each of Pixar's
animated features, and, naturally, over the

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.400
course of those years, screenplays and character
designs go through numerous changes.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.100
In fact, during the production of Cars 3,
artists created over 80,000 storyboards for

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.690
the film, however, only around 10,000 of those
made it into the final movie.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:07.430
But thankfully, sometimes the animators on
a new movie can make use of the good work

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.220
done by their colleagues on a previous Pixar
film.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:15.810
For example, when you look at the sky in Cars
3, 85% of those clouds you see actually came

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.420
from the cloud library for The Good Dinosaur.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:24.210
Although, sadly, screen legend Paul Newman
died back in 2008, his Cars character Doc

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.350
Hudson still plays a crucial part in the new
movie.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.130
And Doc is voiced once again by Newman himself
thanks to many hours of conversations between

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.340
the renowned actor and Cars director John
Lasseter that were caught on tape during voice

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.200
recording sessions for the first movie.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.119
A lot of what Newman and Lasseter talked about
between takes was racing-related, so when

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:47.150
the filmmakers trawled through those recordings
looking for material that would work for Cars

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:51.070
3, they had a veritable treasure-trove of
dialogue to choose from.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:56.010
When the animators were working out how Lightning
McQueen's face should move, they looked to

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.060
Owen Wilson, the voice actor behind the car,
for inspiration.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.830
Which means that the way Lightning moves his
mouth in the movie is based on the shape of

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:08.700
Owen Wilson's mouth when he talks, including
the way the actor curls and purses his lips.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.310
The movie's design team wanted the look of
next-gen racer Jackson Storm to be in complete

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.579
contrast to the look of Lightning McQueen.

00:02:15.579 --> 00:02:20.180
So, they went for a sharp, angular design
for Jackson which stands out compared to Lightning's

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.029
more rounded, flowing look.

00:02:22.029 --> 00:02:26.450
In fact, Jackson Storm is actually how the
Pixar team think a stock-car racer will look

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.849
20 years from now.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:32.790
As for the "S" symbol on Jackson's side, that's
based on the international hurricane symbol

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:37.300
which has been tweaked, stretched and colour
matched to give him a sleek and bold look,

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.340
and to contrast with the lightning bolt on
number 95.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.309
For the appearance of school bus Miss Fritter,
the filmmakers took a subtle style cue from

00:02:45.309 --> 00:02:49.489
Orange Is The New Black star Lea DeLaria who
voices the demolition derby champ.

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:53.010
Yes, just look at Miss Fritter's face and
you'll notice that the animators increased

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.599
the thick rubber trim around her windshield
in a hat tip to DeLaria's trademark thick-rimmed

00:02:57.599 --> 00:02:58.819
glasses.

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:02.620
On top of that, Miss Fritter's smokestacks
are another cool easter egg as their design

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.569
is based on the horns of the classic Disney
character Maleficent, who featured in the

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:12.709
1959 animation Sleeping Beauty as well as
the 2014 live-action movie starring Angelina

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:13.709
Jolie.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:17.769
By the way, for more awesome easter eggs in
Cars 3, tap the top right of the screen to

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:20.299
check out my video on that.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:24.639
When it came to bringing to life the movie's
crazy demolition derby, with all its mud and

00:03:24.639 --> 00:03:29.299
dust flying everywhere, the filmmakers discovered
how difficult it was to make computer-animated

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:33.900
mud that was the right consistency so it looked
realistic but didn't totally cover up the

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:34.970
characters.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:39.089
To give you an idea of just how big a task
this was for the artists and technicians working

00:03:39.089 --> 00:03:44.739
on Cars 3, there are actually 616 shots in
the movie that feature mud, splatters, sand,

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:46.030
dust, and dirt.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:49.389
One of the things the filmmakers did to ensure
they got the right design and feel for the

00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:52.870
scene overall was to visit a night race on
a dirt track.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.680
After that, it was a matter of trial and error
to get the mud looking less like either cake

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:01.260
icing or liquid chocolate, which is how it
looked in early tests, and more like the combination

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.279
of chunky oatmeal and soup that director Brian
Fee wanted.

00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:09.650
When Cars 3 production designer Jay Shuster
joined Pixar back in the early 2000s, one

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.310
of the first things he had to do was design
a pay phone that could function for a large

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.650
mechanical object that had no hands like a
car.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.180
Shuster's pay phone design ended up being
cut from the first Cars movie, however, in

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.310
a little nod to the production designer, the
pay phone has been re-introduced in Cars 3,

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.780
so look out for it during the scene at Flo's
V8 Café.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:33.230
A chance encounter with a Vintage Car rally
during a meeting the Cars 3 team had away

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:37.590
from their Pixar offices gave the filmmakers
the idea to pay tribute to the history of

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:40.530
racing and early NASCAR racers in their movie.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.710
Among the characters in the film based on
real-life NASCAR legends is Louise "Barnstormer"

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:49.940
Nash who is named after the 'first lady of
racing', Louise Smith, who won 38 races in

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:56.850
the 1940s and 50s, and whose Nash Ambassador
car lends its number, 94, to her Cars 3 counterpart.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:01.440
NASCAR legend Wendell Scott, who was the first
African-American to win in the Grand National

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.450
Series, also inspired the Cars 3 team to create
a new character called River Scott, who races

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.290
under number 34, just like Wendell Scott did.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:14.030
Also, Doc Hudson's old coach and friend, Smokey,
who Lightning McQueen turns to for help in

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:19.370
his own career, is based on real-life racing
legend, the innovative mechanic Henry "Smokey"

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:23.290
Yunick, who, just like his name-sake in the
movie, ran an automobile repair shop that

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.350
he dubbed 'the Best Damn Garage in Town'.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.760
Oh, and to emphasise the bond in the movie
between Smokey and Doc Hudson, who was a Hudson

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.550
Hornet, the animators based Smokey's design
on a Hudson pick-up truck.

